WHY THE AGPL, AND WHAT IT MEANS FOR YOU
---------------------------------------

Almost everything anybody does with this is running it. You clone it, you point
it at your own printer, your friends send you messages. Nothing in the AGPL
asks anything of you for that. Change it, break it, rewrite half of it: it is
yours, and nobody is owed anything.

The one thing it does ask is this: if you run a MODIFIED version as a service
that other people use over a network, they are entitled to your source. That is
the whole point of the "Affero" part, and it is why this licence and not MIT.
The alternative was watching somebody take a project made to be shared and turn
it into a closed product, and there is no version of that anybody wanted.

If you are not sure whether that applies to you: it almost certainly does not.
Running it unmodified for your friends is not distribution. Running your own
changed copy for your friends means those friends could ask for the changes -
and if they are your friends, you will just send them.


TRADEMARKS, WHICH THE LICENCE DOES NOT COVER
--------------------------------------------

The AGPL covers copyright. It says nothing about names.

This project grew out of a service called Printomatic, which is a protected
name and is NOT licensed here. Neither is its branding, its wordmark, or its
visual identity. You are free to use this code to run whatever you like; you
are not free to call it Printomatic, or to present your deployment as being
that service.

"Print on my desk" is likewise the name of this project. Fork it, change it,
run it - and please give your own thing its own name. It is better for you
anyway: this is a project whose whole appeal is that it is somebody's own
printer, on somebody's own desk, and it should have somebody's own name on it.


WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
-----------------------------

Two things were removed on the way out of the original repository, and both
removals are load-bearing rather than tidiness:

  * The word list (worker/src/terms.js). A published list of exactly which
    terms a filter catches is a published list of what to type to get past it.
    What ships is the matching ENGINE - the confusable folding, the tokenising,
    the allowlist that keeps ordinary words out of trouble - plus a starter
    list of a dozen entries. Write your own, and once yours is any good, keep
    it out of your public repository too.

  * The payment webhook. The queue's priority mechanism is here
    (worker/src/supporters.js): some tickets can jump ahead of others. How a
    ticket earns that is up to you.


THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL
--------------------

Fonts are not committed to this repository. tools/build_font.py downloads the
one you ask for and prints where it came from. All three presets are under the
SIL Open Font License:

  Google Sans Code   https://github.com/google/fonts/tree/main/ofl/googlesanscode
  JetBrains Mono     https://github.com/JetBrains/JetBrainsMono
  Courier Prime      https://github.com/quoteunquoteapps/CourierPrime

The two web fonts that ARE committed - Kode Mono and Doto, in web/fonts - carry
their OFL text alongside them in the same directory.

The proof-of-work challenge format is Altcha's, reimplemented here rather than
vendored; Altcha itself is MIT licensed.
